  "body": "Hi all, first time contributor here.\r\n\r\nI felt this was timely due to the recent work that Microsoft and others have done to improve the default styling of inputs elements across multiple browsers. I didn't want to hijack https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/4180 - so I'm opening a separate issue.\r\n\r\nI created a proof of concept (chromium-based browsers only) that may be [seen here](https://codepen.io/alystair/pen/jOWWqwa?editors=1100) for styling of checkbox inputs with a 'toggle' attribute. My preference leans towards 'toggle' naming instead of 'switch'. @annevk's idea of using `appearance` CSS property feels OK as well - will edit title if this is the pref. method moving forward.\r\n\r\nhttps://codepen.io/alystair/pen/jOWWqwa?editors=1100\r\n\r\n# About the POC\r\n- Matches default Chrome style\r\n- Pure HTML/CSS with no JS outside of setting `indeterminate`.\r\n- Although it's technically a checkbox, it stylistically resembles an elongated radio input.\r\n- POC won't work under Firefox because according to the spec, I shouldn't be able to use ::before/after on self-closing elements(?), it saved me plenty of time!\r\n- It's also fully accessible... because it's literally a checkbox input.\r\n- Unsure under what license it should be released under so it may be reused/repurposed/reproduced, please let me know!\r\n\r\nSince it's an alternative styling to 'checkbox', implementation wouldn't be as difficult as creating an entirely new input type as proposed in #4180.\r\n\r\nWould love comments/crits!\r\n\r\n//EDIT// Fixed POC link",
